<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<appendix xml:id="hkg-notation-index">
  <title>Notation</title>
  <section xml:id="hkg-notation-metric-periods">
    <title>Metrics and periods</title>
    <p><dl>
      <li><title><m>X,M,2n</m></title><p>An IHS manifold, its underlying smooth manifold, and its dimension; <m>\dim_\R M=4n</m>. <xref ref="hkg-notation"/>, <xref ref="hkg-def-ihs"/>.</p></li>
      <li><title><m>g,I,J,K;\ \omega_L</m></title><p>A hyperkähler metric and parallel quaternionic triple; <m>\omega_L(u,v)=g(Lu,v)</m> for an induced complex structure <m>L</m>. <xref ref="hkg-def-hyperkahler-metric"/>, <xref ref="hkg-lemma-parallel-complex"/>.</p></li>
      <li><title><m>\sigma</m></title><p>A holomorphic symplectic two-form in the geometric chapters. In stable-object moduli, <m>\sigma=(Z,\mathcal P)</m> is instead a stability condition. <xref ref="hkg-def-holomorphic-symplectic"/>, <xref ref="hkg-def-bridgeland-stability"/>.</p></li>
      <li><title><m>I_\zeta,X_\zeta,Z</m></title><p>The induced complex structure, the fibre <m>X_\zeta=(M,I_\zeta)</m>, and the twistor space <m>Z\to\PP^1</m>. <xref ref="hkg-eq-induced-coordinate"/>, <xref ref="hkg-def-twistor-space"/>.</p></li>
      <li><title><m>P_g</m></title><p>For a compact hyperkähler metric of holonomy <m>\Sp(n)</m>, the oriented positive real three-plane spanned by <m>[\omega_I],[\omega_J],[\omega_K]</m>. <xref ref="hkg-twistor-period-conic"/>.</p></li>
      <li><title><m>L_\zeta</m></title><p>The oriented period two-plane <m>\R\operatorname{Re}[\sigma_\zeta]\oplus\R\operatorname{Im}[\sigma_\zeta]</m> inside <m>P_g</m>. <xref ref="hkg-twistor-period-conic"/>.</p></li>
      <li><title><m>\operatorname{Def}(X)</m></title><p>The Kuranishi germ of deformations of <m>X</m> with identified central fibre. <xref ref="hkg-deformation-families"/>.</p></li>
      <li><title><m>\eta,\Lambda</m></title><p>A marking <m>\eta:H^2(X,\Z)\to\Lambda</m> and its fixed target lattice. <xref ref="hkg-definition-marking"/>.</p></li>
      <li><title><m>\Omega_\Lambda,\operatorname{Per}</m></title><p>The period domain and the period map, which sends a marked manifold to <m>\eta(H^{2,0}(X))</m>. <xref ref="hkg-definition-period-domain"/>, <xref ref="hkg-equation-global-period"/>.</p></li>
    </dl></p>
  </section>
  <section xml:id="hkg-notation-lattices-moduli">
    <title>Lattices and moduli</title>
    <p><dl>
      <li><title><m>q_X</m></title><p>The primitive integral BBF bilinear form; <m>q_X(a)=q_X(a,a)</m>. <xref ref="hkg-bbf-existence"/>.</p></li>
      <li><title><m>L^\vee,A_L,\bar q_L</m></title><p>The dual lattice, the finite group <m>L^\vee/L</m>, and its quadratic form with values in <m>\Q/2\Z</m> when <m>L</m> is even. <xref ref="hkg-def-discriminant-group"/>.</p></li>
      <li><title><m>\Mon^2(X),O^+</m></title><p>Degree-two parallel-transport operators from <m>X</m> to itself, and the group preserving the positive orientation. The first is a subgroup of the second. <xref ref="hkg-definition-parallel-transport"/>, <xref ref="hkg-orientation-transport-anchor" text="custom">positive orientation</xref>.</p></li>
      <li><title><m>\mathcal I_X</m></title><p>For <m>\mathrm{K3}^{[n]}</m> type, <m>n\geq2</m>, the distinguished <m>O(\widetilde\Lambda)</m>-orbit of primitive isometric embeddings of <m>H^2(X,\Z)</m> into the Mukai lattice. <xref ref="hkg-markman-embedding-orbit"/>.</p></li>
      <li><title><m>\delta</m></title><p>On <m>S^{[n]}</m>, <m>n\geq2</m>, half the reduced Hilbert–Chow exceptional divisor class: <m>[E]=2\delta</m>, <m>q(\delta)=-2(n-1)</m>. <xref ref="hkg-eq-hilbert-lattice"/>.</p></li>
      <li><title><m>S^{[n]},K_n(A)</m></title><p>The Hilbert scheme of length-<m>n</m> subschemes and the sum-zero fibre in <m>A^{[n+1]}</m>, respectively. Both have dimension <m>2n</m>. <xref ref="hkg-def-hilbert-points"/>, <xref ref="hkg-def-generalized-kummer"/>.</p></li>
      <li><title><m>v(E),v^2</m></title><p>For objects on a surface, the Mukai vector <m>\operatorname{ch}(E)\sqrt{\operatorname{td}}</m> and its Mukai square <m>\langle v,v\rangle</m>. On an abelian surface, <m>v(E)=\operatorname{ch}(E)</m>. <xref ref="hkg-def-mukai"/>, <xref ref="hkg-abelian-moduli"/>.</p></li>
      <li><title><m>N(S),N(A)</m></title><p>The algebraic Mukai lattices <m>\Z\oplus\operatorname{NS}\oplus\Z</m> of a projective K3 or abelian surface. <xref ref="hkg-derived-mukai"/>, <xref ref="hkg-abelian-derived-moduli"/>.</p></li>
      <li><title><m>M_\sigma(v)</m></title><p>Stable-object moduli with a fixed vector and a fixed lift of its phase. In the nonempty cases of the moduli theorems, its dimension is <m>v^2+2</m>. <xref ref="hkg-derived-moduli-existence"/>, <xref ref="hkg-abelian-bridgeland-moduli"/>.</p></li>
      <li><title><m>K_\sigma(v)</m></title><p>The Albanese fibre for objects on an abelian surface. Under the primitive-vector and genericity hypotheses of <xref ref="hkg-abelian-bridgeland-moduli"/>, with <m>v^2\geq6</m>, its dimension is <m>v^2-2</m> and its second cohomology is Mukai-isometric to <m>v^\perp</m>.</p></li>
    </dl></p>
  </section>
  <section xml:id="hkg-notation-cohomology-chern">
    <title>Cohomology and characteristic classes</title>
    <p><dl>
      <li><title><m>H^k,b_k,h^{p,q}</m></title><p>Singular cohomology, Betti numbers, and Hodge numbers. The superscript <m>k</m> is ordinary degree; omitted coefficients mean <m>\Q</m>, and <m>H^{p,q}\subset H^{p+q}(X,\C)</m>. <xref ref="hkg-notation"/>, <xref ref="hkg-salamon-relation"/>.</p></li>
      <li><title><m>\operatorname{Pic},\operatorname{NS},\rho</m></title><p>Line bundles, <m>\operatorname{Pic}/\operatorname{Pic}^0</m>, and the rank of this quotient. For IHS manifolds, <m>\operatorname{Pic}^0=0</m>. <xref ref="hkg-notation"/>, <xref ref="hkg-picard-identification"/>.</p></li>
      <li><title><m>c_X,C_X(\eta)</m></title><p>The constants in <m>\int_Xa^{2n}=c_Xq_X(a)^n</m> and <m>\int_X\eta a^{2n-2j}=C_X(\eta)q_X(a)^{n-j}</m>. The second requires <m>\eta\in H^{4j}</m>, <m>0\leq j\leq n</m>, whose parallel transport has type <m>(2j,2j)</m> on all small deformations; <m>C_X(1)=c_X</m>. <xref ref="hkg-eq-fujiki"/>, <xref ref="hkg-generalized-fujiki"/>.</p></li>
      <li><title><m>Q_X=q_X^\vee</m></title><p>The degree-four cup-product image of the inverse BBF tensor: <m>Q_X=\sum q^{ij}e_ie_j</m>, with no factor <m>1/2</m>. <xref ref="hkg-inverse-class"/>.</p></li>
      <li><title><m>c_i(E),\operatorname{td}_{2j}(X)</m></title><p>Chern classes have degree <m>2i</m>, with <m>c_i(X)=c_i(T_X)</m>; the indicated Todd component has degree <m>4j</m>. In the Chow chapter, Chern classes also denote the specified Chow classes. <xref ref="hkg-notation"/>, <xref ref="hkg-rr-polynomial"/>, <xref ref="hkg-def-distinguished-chow-rings"/>.</p></li>
      <li><title><m>\Delta(E)</m></title><p>The discriminant <m>2r c_2(E)-(r-1)c_1(E)^2</m> of a rank-<m>r</m> bundle. Its <m>SU(2)</m>-invariance characterizes projective hyperholomorphicity under the polystability hypothesis. <xref ref="hkg-lubke-norm-identity"/>, <xref ref="hkg-projective-hyperholomorphic-discriminant"/>.</p></li>
      <li><title><m>\operatorname{RR}_X(t)</m></title><p>The Riemann–Roch polynomial, satisfying <m>\chi(X,L)=\operatorname{RR}_X(q_X(c_1L))</m>. <xref ref="hkg-rr-formula"/>.</p></li>
      <li><title><m>\SH^k(X)</m></title><p>The degree-<m>k</m> part of the subalgebra generated by <m>H^2(X,\Q)</m>, with ordinary cohomological grading; <m>\SH^{2j}</m> is the image of <m>\operatorname{Sym}^jH^2</m>. <xref ref="hkg-def-verbitsky-subalgebra"/>.</p></li>
      <li><title><m>L_a,\Lambda_a,h</m></title><p>Cup product by <m>a</m>, its Lefschetz lowering operator when defined, and <m>h|_{H^k}=(k-2n)\operatorname{id}</m>. <xref ref="hkg-llv-definition"/>, <xref ref="hkg-def-lefschetz-class"/>.</p></li>
      <li><title><m>\mathfrak g,\mathfrak g_0</m></title><p>The Lie subalgebra of <m>\operatorname{End}_{\C}H^*(X,\C)</m> generated by the Lefschetz triples, and its subalgebra <m>\{D\in\mathfrak g:[h,D]=0\}</m>. <xref ref="hkg-def-llv-algebra"/>.</p></li>
      <li><title><m>\overline{\mathfrak g}</m></title><p>The reduced LLV subalgebra <m>[\mathfrak g_0,\mathfrak g_0]</m>, identified by restriction with <m>\mathfrak{so}(H^2(X,\C),q_X)</m>. <xref ref="hkg-eq-reduced-llv-definition"/>, <xref ref="hkg-llv-structure"/>.</p></li>
      <li><title><m>W,Q,e_\pm</m></title><p>The auxiliary orthogonal space <m>W=\C e_-\oplus H^2(X,\C)\oplus\C e_+</m>, with <m>Q(e_-,e_+)=1</m> and <m>Q|_{H^2}=q_X</m>. The respective <m>h</m>-weights are <m>-2,0,2</m>. <xref ref="hkg-def-llv-quadratic-space"/>, <xref ref="hkg-llv-structure"/>.</p></li>
    </dl></p>
  </section>
  <section xml:id="hkg-notation-cycles-degenerations">
    <title>Cones, cycles, and degenerations</title>
    <p><dl>
      <li><title><m>\mathcal C_X,\mathcal K_X,\mathcal{BK}_X</m></title><p>The positive cone, Kähler cone, and union of pullbacks of Kähler cones of birational IHS models. <xref ref="hkg-positive-cone"/>, <xref ref="hkg-birational-kahler-cone"/>.</p></li>
      <li><title><m>\operatorname{Nef}(X),\operatorname{Mov}(X)</m></title><p>Divisor nef classes and the cone generated by movable integral divisors; <m>\overline{\operatorname{Mov}}(X)</m> denotes its closure. <xref ref="hkg-cones-projectivity"/>, <xref ref="hkg-birational-identification"/>.</p></li>
      <li><title><m>\operatorname{Nef}^+(X),\operatorname{Mov}^+(X)</m></title><p>The cones generated by rational points of the nef cone and of the closed movable cone, respectively. <xref ref="hkg-def-rational-hull-cones"/>.</p></li>
      <li><title><m>z_C</m></title><p>The rational BBF dual of a curve: <m>q_X(z_C,a)=\int_Ca</m>. <xref ref="hkg-no-curves-kahler"/>.</p></li>
      <li><title><m>\operatorname{CH}^p,\operatorname{CH}_0,\operatorname{cl}</m></title><p>Codimension-<m>p</m> cycles and zero-cycles modulo rational equivalence, and the cycle class map to degree-<m>2p</m> cohomology. A subscript <m>\Q</m> means rational coefficients. <xref ref="hkg-def-chow"/>, <xref ref="hkg-def-cycle-class"/>.</p></li>
      <li><title><m>\operatorname{DCH}^*(X),R^*(X)</m></title><p>The rational Chow subrings generated by divisors, and by divisors together with the Chern classes of <m>T_X</m>, respectively. <xref ref="hkg-def-distinguished-chow-rings"/>.</p></li>
      <li><title><m>F_\nabla,F</m></title><p>In bundle theory, <m>F_\nabla=\nabla^2</m> is curvature, also abbreviated <m>F</m>. In fibration arguments, <m>F=f^{-1}(b)</m> is a fibre. <xref ref="hkg-hyperholomorphic-connections"/>, <xref ref="hkg-lubke-norm-identity"/>, <xref ref="hkg-smooth-fiber-torus"/>.</p></li>
      <li><title><m>T_d,N_d,\nu_d</m></title><p>Monodromy on <m>H^d</m>, its logarithm after unipotent base change, and <m>\nu_d=\max\{j:N_d^j\ne0\}</m> when <m>N_d\ne0</m>, with <m>\nu(0)=0</m>. A block of length <m>r+1</m> has index <m>r</m>. <xref ref="hkg-degeneration-setup"/>, <xref ref="hkg-def-log-monodromy"/>.</p></li>
      <li><title><m>W_\bullet(A)[m]</m></title><p>The nilpotent weight filtration centered at <m>m</m>. For limiting cohomology in degree <m>d</m>, the center of <m>W_\bullet(N_d)[d]</m> is <m>d</m>. <xref ref="hkg-def-nilpotent-weight-filtration"/>, <xref ref="hkg-limiting-mixed-hodge"/>.</p></li>
      <li><title><m>\mathcal W^\beta,P_iH^d</m></title><p>For a projective Lagrangian fibration and an ample rational base class <m>\alpha</m>, let <m>\beta=f^*\alpha</m>. Then <m>\mathcal W^\beta=W_\bullet(L_\beta)[n]</m> has center <m>n</m> on total cohomology. The perverse normalization is <m>P_iH^d=\mathcal W^\beta_{i-d+2n}\cap H^d</m>, with indices <m>0\leq i\leq2n</m>. <xref ref="hkg-compact-perverse-weight"/>, <xref ref="hkg-perverse-cup-description"/>.</p></li>
    </dl></p>
  </section>
</appendix>
